{
  "image_name": "b8a4472c62a180686301d774731d714d.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how sediment moves from land to the sea in the Anthropocene and how human activity has changed the size of these sediment loads. Overall, it shows a source-to-sink system in which weathering and hilltop erosion in mountains feed rivers, which carry most material seaward. In 2010, fluvial transport is the dominant pathway, with smaller added inputs from wind-blown dust, coastal erosion, subglacial meltwater, and iceberg-rafted debris, producing a total land-to-sea sediment load of about 17.3 Gt per year.\n\nThe lower part compares sediment-related loads in 1950 and 2010 and makes the main point that human mobilization of sediment has greatly increased over time. Nearly every category grows by 2010, especially soil erosion, coal and mineral mining, aggregate mining, construction, dredging, and trawling. Reservoir sequestration and “other consumption” also rise strongly, indicating that people not only mobilize more sediment but also trap or redirect more of it inland. The total anthropogenic sediment load increases dramatically from about 70 Gt per year in 1950 to nearly 300 Gt per year in 2010. Visually, the figure emphasizes that human activities now rival or exceed many natural sediment-transfer processes.",
